---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/alternatives/activecampaign-alternative';
const title = "ActiveCampaign alternatives: replace campaigns without losing sales authority";
const desc = "Decide whether to repair ActiveCampaign or replace Shopify marketing while retaining account, quote, contact and approval ownership.";
const faqs = [{"question": "Does leaving ActiveCampaign require replacing the CRM?", "answer": "No. Decide separately who owns accounts, quotes and approvals, then demonstrate how those decisions reach marketing eligibility."}, {"question": "Can ActiveCampaign send SMS?", "answer": "It offers an SMS add-on. Confirm plan eligibility, supported destinations and the required functions in the actual quote."}, {"question": "How should a waiting automation migrate?", "answer": "Inventory its current position and completed actions. Assign one sender, cancel old pending work explicitly, and re-check eligibility before enabling the destination."}];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} dateModified="2026-11-25" />
  <article class="container py-16 prose-shopify">
    <a href="/alternatives">All alternatives</a>
    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <p>Start with the work that would disappear in a switch: who owns the account, which buyer has an open quote, and who can authorize a promotion? A simpler campaign editor is valuable only if these decisions still reach the sending queue. The procurement and migration exercises below are proposed acceptance criteria, not performed tests or merchant results.</p>
    <h2>Repair first when the CRM earns its place</h2>
    <p>Keep ActiveCampaign on the shortlist when sales and marketing share account decisions. Its <a href="https://help.activecampaign.com/hc/en-us/articles/14251311251356-Overview-of-ActiveCampaign-plans">current plan overview</a> describes enhanced CRM and SMS add-ons, plus plan-dependent custom permissions. The claim that it has no SMS offering is obsolete. Check the actual base plan, seats, add-ons, destinations and usage in your quote. Evidence checked November 25, 2026.</p><p>A repair proposal might retire unused branches, give every live automation an owner, and separate rep tasks from promotional sends. Compare that scoped repair cost with replacement. An unused pipeline is a reason to simplify; a pipeline that controls wholesale pricing needs an explicit successor.</p>
    <h2>Shortlist by the decision being moved</h2>
    <p><a href="/apps/klaviyo">Klaviyo</a> is a candidate when Shopify customer and order data should drive marketing. Its <a href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/115005080407">Shopify integration guide</a> describes profile/order synchronization and configuration. This supports a commerce-marketing evaluation, not a promise to replace account approvals or quote management.</p><p><a href="/apps/omnisend">Omnisend</a> is a candidate when one team wants campaign and automation work across email and SMS: its <a href="https://www.omnisend.com/">product overview</a> describes both channels, workflows and purchase-based segmentation. Ask each candidate to demonstrate your account exclusions using actual available fields. Neither a shared contact record nor an email subscription proves SMS eligibility. Keep a separate sales system only if a named owner can maintain the handoff.</p>
    <h2>Write the account-to-contact contract</h2>
    <p>List account ID, contact ID, Shopify customer ID, quote ID/version, quote expiry, account approval, rep owner and channel permission as separate fields. Specify source, allowed values, update time and conflict authority for each. Two contacts at one account can be buyer and approver; a third may be email-only. Decide whether an open quote suppresses an account-wide discount or only the quoted buyer. A tag without an accountable source is insufficient evidence.</p>
    <h2>Proposed acceptance: approval changes during a queued wait</h2>
    <ol><li>Create a synthetic account with buyer, approver and unrelated retail customer records. Queue a promotional email and an independently permitted SMS for the buyer.</li><li>Open a quote after entry but before send. Inspect whether the waiting actions re-check eligibility; do not infer cancellation from a changed segment.</li><li>Change the quote version, revoke approval, move the rep and record a purchase. Expect only the explicitly scoped records to exit; the unrelated customer remains eligible.</li><li>Replay an older approval and a duplicate event. Require version handling and one auditable outcome. If the connector cannot carry the decision, assign a supported manual hold with an owner before launch.</li></ol>
    <h2>Role authority and the caller who needs an answer</h2>
    <p>Have the rep identify who can approve price exceptions, the marketer identify who can release sends, and the administrator identify who can edit mappings. Use the intended restricted roles in the proposed demonstration. A caller asking why their quote changed needs a rep or fallback owner who can see the quote version and message record. Do not grant every user admin access to make a pilot pass. If the sales owner is absent, define who can hold the queue and who can authorize re-entry.</p>
    <h2>Rebuild templates and permissions deliberately</h2>
    <p>Inventory active flows, entry/exit rules, delays, branches, forms, suppression records and template assets before choosing scope. Rebuild a quote follow-up and a retail welcome first, including missing-name fallbacks, product links and reply routing. Check rendering and sender authentication in the destination. Exportability does not establish equivalent field types, automation semantics or asset rights. Preserve source/timestamp/disclosure and channel-specific opt-outs; quarantine records whose permission cannot be established.</p>
    <h2>Cutover is a controlled transfer of authority</h2>
    <p>Choose one sender per flow and cohort. Reconcile waiting contacts and completed actions at a recorded cutoff; decide who cancels old queued work and who verifies it stopped. Follow the destination integration sequence: Klaviyo’s guide specifically warns about duplicate opt-in emails from leaving a prior ESP connected. Do not run both integrations on the same audience as a default pilot. Keep a read-only baseline and an exclusion ledger so a rollback cannot replay completed messages.</p>
    <h2>Compare repair and replacement economics</h2>
    <p>Request quotes for current and peak contact counts, seats, CRM/SMS add-ons, destination mix, message segments, carrier charges, integration upkeep and overlap time. Add rep retraining and rebuilding approvals to replacement cost. For a proposed controlled cohort comparison, use the same period and eligibility rules: contribution per assigned eligible customer equals net sales after discounts/refunds, less goods, fulfillment, messaging and allocated platform/support costs, divided by assigned eligible customers. Compare with a holdout where feasible; attributed revenue alone does not establish lift. Subtract discounts and refunds once.</p>
    <h2>Release and rollback evidence</h2>
    <p>Before release, require signed field mappings, restricted-role checks, template previews, queue inspection, permission counts and a named reply owner. Define acceptable delay and discrepancy thresholds before the exercise. Stop if a revoked account approval still permits a promotion. Roll back by pausing destination entry and pending sends, reconciling delivered actions, then restoring only unsent eligible work in the former system. An export file alone cannot restore queue position or sales authority.</p>
    <h2>Choose the smallest justified change</h2>
    <p>Repair ActiveCampaign if account-linked automation is useful and the defects are ownership or configuration. Replace marketing if a candidate passes the commerce workflow and the retained sales authority has a dependable interface. Replace the CRM too only after the quote/account demonstration passes separately. Use the <a href="/apps/activecampaign">ActiveCampaign review</a> for product scope and the <a href="/guides/pricing">cost worksheet</a> for the channel budget; no supplier is a default winner.</p>
    <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
